  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 450 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe long-term forecast period will be characterized primarily by a \nsharp increase in overall rain chances as well as a general decrease \nin afternoon high temperatures amid more widespread cloud cover and \nconvection. An upper trough will dig into the eastern CONUS by \nSaturday, sending a surface low into the Ohio Valley and its \nattendant frontal boundary into the forecast area. This will send \ndeep tropical moisture streaming quickly back northward into the \narea after the brief reprieve we will have experienced late this \nweek. With PWATs forecast to surge back into the 2 - 2.25\" range by \nSaturday, widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected. While \nthe severe threat currently looks rather limited, locally heavy \nrainfall will be a concern given the environment, and as such WPC \nalready has a \"Marginal\" Excessive Rainfall out for our area on \nSaturday.\n\nThe front is progged to make gradual progress into the area on \nSunday, keeping PoPs quite high in advance. Again, locally heavy \nrainfall will remain the primary concern. Additionally, a weak upper \nlow looks develop within the trough axis that will bisect the \nforecast area by late weekend into early next week. As such, while \nthe surface front will gradually push eastward and wash out, the \npresence of the weak meandering upper low should provide sufficient \nlift for continued scattered diurnally-enhanced convection through \nearly next week before upper riding looks to make a gradual return \nthereafter.\n\nAs mentioned above, more widespread cloud cover/rainfall will keep \nhigh temperatures more tempered in the 80s to perhaps low 90s, \nthough overnight lows will creep back into the 70s.",
